implementationPlan.description:
TESTS + IMPLEMENTATION: resolveImport and evaluateArchitecture, in architectureRules.ts and architectureRules.test.ts. Report both in modifiedFiles.

resolveImport(fromPath, specifier, treePaths):
- First replace backslashes in the specifier with '/', because Windows CommonJS resolves them as separators.
- It is RELATIVE iff it is '.' or '..', or starts with './' or '../'. Anything else returns null as external: bare packages, node: builtins, '@scope/x', absolute paths and aliases like 'src/x'.
- Join it onto fromPath's directory segment by segment, without node:path. '' and '.' segments are skipped and '..' pops; popping past the repo root returns null.
- Candidates, in order:
  - If the specifier ends in '.js': stem + '.ts', '.tsx', '.js' and '.mjs'.
  - Otherwise: the joined path itself, then joined + each of those four extensions, then joined + '/index' + each of the four. When the join is the repo root, only the 'index' candidates apply.
- The first candidate found in treePaths wins (exact, case-sensitive Set.has). If none matches, return null.
- A module-private isRelative() (with the same backslash rule) is shared with the evaluator. Export nothing beyond the documented names.

evaluateArchitecture(rules, changed, treePaths):
- Error handling, fail-closed: each changed[i].path must satisfy normalizeAffectedFile(path, 'changed[i].path') === path. Otherwise throw invalidInput('changed[i].path', 'must be normalized'); a mis-normalized path would otherwise silently select no rule.
- Paths must be unique. A duplicate throws invalidInput on the later index, 'must be unique'.
- covers(prefix, p) is p === prefix || p.startsWith(prefix + '/'). Coverage compares whole segments, so 'src/state' does not cover 'src/statement.ts'.
- A rule is SELECTED iff any of its `from` prefixes covers any changed path, of any extension.
- SCANNED files are the changed paths that end, case-sensitively, in .ts, .tsx, .js, .mjs or .cjs and that a selected rule's from covers.
  - Extract each file once, cached by path.
  - The text of a changed path that is not scanned is never read; '' is fine there.
- For each ref, resolved = resolveImport(file, specifier, treePaths):
  - null with a relative specifier gives uncertainty {file, line, reason: 'unresolved-relative'};
  - null with an external specifier is skipped;
  - otherwise, each selected rule whose from covers THIS file and whose forbid covers resolved gives a violation {ruleId, file, line, specifier, resolved}.
  A rule's forbid never applies outside its own from.
- Every extracted uncertainty of a scanned file is surfaced as {file, line, reason}.
- Build objects in the documented key order: the result is {outcome, selectedRules, violations, uncertain}.
- Sort by code-unit comparison only, never localeCompare:
  - selectedRules by id;
  - violations by file, then line, ruleId, specifier and resolved;
  - uncertain by file, then line and reason.
- outcome is 'failed' if there is any violation, else 'inconclusive' if there is any uncertainty, else 'passed'.

TESTS, `describe('resolveImport')` and `describe('evaluateArchitecture')`, with exact toStrictEqual:
- V1 resolve:
  - '.js' to '.ts' wins over an existing '.js';
  - '.js' to '.tsx', to '.js' itself, and to '.mjs';
  - './dir' to 'dir/index.ts';
  - extensionless './b' to 'b.ts';
  - './data.json' exact;
  - '..' and '../x/y.js' climbing;
  - '../../x.js' from 'a/b.ts' gives null;
  - 'vitest', 'node:fs', '@s/p' and 'src/tools/t.js' give null;
  - an unmatched relative gives null;
  - a backslash-separated '../tools/t.js' resolves.
- V2 selection:
  - a rule from ['src/state'] with only 'docs/a.md' changed gives selectedRules [] and 'passed';
  - changed 'src/statement.ts' is not selected;
  - with two rules, only the covering rule is selected;
  - a changed src/state/README.md holding import text is selected, not scanned, and passes.
- V3 violation: src/state/s.ts imports '../tools/t.js' with src/tools/t.ts in treePaths. The result is 'failed', with the exact finding including line and resolved 'src/tools/t.ts'.
- V4 scope: rule A has from src/state and forbid src/tools; rule B has from src/ui and forbid src/db. src/state/x.ts importing src/db gives no violation.
- V5 uncertainty: a dynamic import, a non-literal require and an unresolved './missing.js' each give 'inconclusive' with exact entries.
- V6 precedence:
  - a violation plus uncertainty gives 'failed', with the uncertainty still listed;
  - uncertainty only gives 'inconclusive';
  - neither gives 'passed'.
- V7 determinism: shuffled changed and rule order give byte-identical JSON.stringify results.
- V8 input errors: './src/a.ts' and a backslash path give INVALID_INPUT with context.field 'changed[0].path'. A duplicate gives 'changed[1].path'.

ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW, once, as the final step. Load adversarial-self-review and read the diff as an attacker trying to land a forbidden import past this check.

1. Hiding an import:
   - every JS line terminator ends `//`;
   - escaped identifiers and specifiers;
   - `...` versus `.`;
   - code inside ${};
   - a regex holding quotes, `//` or a backtick;
   - CRLF;
   - a .d.ts file (it ends in .ts, so it is scanned).

2. Pathological input:
   - one linear pass with no rescans; the clause scan stops at import/export, so `import import import ...` stays linear;
   - no backtracking regex over the raw text;
   - a 1 MB single line, deep ${ nesting and unterminated constructs all return without throwing.

3. Empty inputs: '' text, no changed files, and an empty treePaths, under which relative imports are unresolved and the outcome is inconclusive.

4. Determinism: code-unit sorts and fixed key order. No output depends on Set or Map iteration order.

5. Purity: exactly the two util imports, no node: import, no IO and no module state.

6. Rails:
   - no forbidden-pattern tokens in code or tests, including the prototype-key token; write no test for that key;
   - no .moe edits and no Git writes;
   - verified-local untouched.

Record the accepted residuals in the step note:
- JSX text is lexed as code, with line-local recovery;
- a regex right after `)` reads as division;
- non-relative path aliases are external;
- runtime indirection (an aliased require, createRequire) is out of lexical reach.

Write the Serena memory 'task-task-2cd62d2c87ab4e118222a5a8b841db6c-handoff' with the landed names, the fail-closed INVALID_INPUT on changed paths (V10 must map it to exit 2), and those residuals for V10 (task-db328cda).

implementationPlan.note:
Adversarial review.
- FIXED, a real bypass: legacy octal escapes. `require('\056\056/tools/t.js')` loads ../tools in sloppy CJS, but the decoder turned `\0` into NUL, so the import read as external. Now decoded and tested; a mutant without it fails X5.
- ADDED tests:
  - X5: regex rows holding `//` and a backtick, and a `[//]` class;
  - X6 pathological, which must stay linear: 300 KB `+/[`, 100k nested `${`, 200k chained `import`, a 1 MB identifier, a 1 MB unterminated string, 300k failed `\u{` escapes;
  - X6 seeded fuzz: 3000 runs never throw and keep lines inside the text;
  - V9: .d.ts, .cjs and .tsx are scanned; .mts, .jsx, .TS and .json are not.
  The no-regexOffUntil mutant fails X6 (vitest timeout, about 20s).
- Checklist:
  - hiding: any line terminator ends `//`; escapes, spread, ${}, CRLF and .d.ts are all covered;
  - empty inputs: '', [] changed and an empty tree are covered;
  - determinism: code-unit sorts with full keys, fixed key order, no output from Set or Map iteration;
  - purity: exactly the two util imports, no node:, no IO, only readonly module constants;
  - rails: a forbidden-token grep over both files has no hits; no .moe edits, no Git writes, verified-local untouched.
- GATE re-run fresh, HEAD b51ff41 plus overlay: npm test exit 0 (181 files; 5041 passed, 16 pre-existing skips); tsc exit 0; cmp ok.
- Accepted residuals:
  - JSX text is lexed as code; a stray backtick or `/*` in it can swallow lines;
  - a regex after a non-control `)` reads as division;
  - a division misread as a regex can hide a same-line require between slashes: after an expression-ending `}`, postfix ++/--, TS `!`, or of/yield/await as identifiers;
  - non-relative aliases are external;
  - runtime indirection is out of reach: aliased require, createRequire, module.require, require.call/apply, (require)(...);
  - .mts/.cts/.jsx are not scanned, and a `.d.ts`-only target is unresolved, so the result is inconclusive;
  - paths over 500 characters throw INVALID_INPUT.
- Handoff memory written for V10.
